ses L135:     * cite125†Latest Releases L136:     * cite126†Major Economic Indicators L137:     * cite127†Schedules for news Releases L138:     * cite128†By Month L139:     * cite129†By News Release L140:     * cite130†Current Year L141:     * cite131†Prior Years L142:     * cite132†Archived News Releases L143:     * cite133†Employment & Unemployment L144:     * cite133†Monthly L145:     * cite133†Quarterly, annual, and other L146:     * cite134†Inflation & Prices L147:     * cite135†Pay & Benefits & Workplace Injuries L148:     * cite136†Productivity & Technology L149:     * cite137†Employment Projections L150:     * cite138†Regional News Releases L151:   * cite139†Classroom L152: \nL153:     * cite139†K-12 Student & Teacher Resources L154:     * cite140†Games & Quizzes L155:     * cite141†Student's Desk L156:     * cite142†Teacher's Desk L157:     * cite143†Questions & Answers L158: \nL159: cite124†Economic News Release L160: \nL161: cite144†PRINT:†data.bls.gov L162: \nL163: cite145†CPS L164: \nL165: Labor Force Statistics from the Current Population Survey\nL166: \nL167:   * cite52†CPS Homepage L168:   * cite146†CPS Overview L169:   * cite147†CPS FAQ L170:   * cite148†Contact CPS L171: \nL172: [Button: Close]\nL173: \nL174: cite149†CES L175: \nL176: Current Employment Statistics - CES (National)\nL177:   * cite43†CES Homepage L178:   * cite150†CES Overview L179:   * cite151†CES FAQs L180:   * cite152†Contact CES L181: \nL182: [Button: Close]\nL183: # Employment Situation News Release\nL184: \nL185:     Transmission of material in this news release is embargoed until                       USDL-26-1435\nL186:     8:30 a.m. (ET) Friday, September 4, 2026\nL187: \nL188:     Technical information:\nL189:      Household data:      (202) 691-6378  *  cpsinfo@bls.gov  *  www.bls.gov/cps\nL190:      Establishment data:  (202) 691-6555  *  cesinfo@bls.gov  *  www.bls.gov/ces\nL191: \nL192:     Media contact:        (202) 691-5902  *  PressOffice@bls.gov\nL193:                                 THE EMPLOYMENT SITUATION - AUGUST 2026\nL194: \nL195: \nL196:     Total nonfarm payroll employment increased by 162,000 in August, and the unemployment rate was\nL197:     unchanged at 4.1 percent, the U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics reported today. Employment\nL198:     increased in food services and drinking places and in local government education. The\nL199:     information industry lost jobs.\nL200: \nL201:     This news release presents statistics from two monthly surveys. The household survey measures\nL202:     labor force status, including unemployment, by demographic characteristics. The establishment\nL203:     survey measures nonfarm employment, hours, and earnings by industry. For more information\nL204:     about the concepts and statistical methodology used in these two surveys, see the Technical\nL205:     Note.\nL206: \nL207:     Household Survey Data\nL208: \nL209:     The unemployment rate was unchanged at 4.1 percent in August, and the number of unemployed\nL210:     people changed little at 7.0 million. Both measures changed little over the year.\nL211:     (See table A-1.)\nL212: \nL213:     Among the major worker groups, the unemployment rate for people who are Asian declined to 3.2\nL214:     percent in August. The rate for teenagers edged up to 14.1 percent over the month, mostly\nL215:     offsetting a decline in the prior month. The jobless rates for adult men (4.0 percent), adult\nL216:     women (3.5 percent), and people who are White (3.7 percent), Black (6.0 percent), or Hispanic\nL217:     (4.8 percent) showed little change in August. (See tables A-1, A-2, and A-3.)\nL218: \nL219:     The number of long-term unemployed (those jobless for 27 weeks or more) changed little at 1.9\nL220:     million in August. The long-term unemployed accounted for 27.0 percent of all unemployed\nL221:     people. (See table A-12.)\nL222: \nL223:     The labor force participation rate edged up to 61.6 percent in August but is down by 0.5\nL224:     percentage point since January. The employment-population ratio, at 59.1 percent, changed\nL225:     little over the month and since January. (See table A-1.)\nL226: \nL227:     The number of people employed part time for economic reasons decreased by 414,000 to 4.4\nL228:     million in August. These individuals would have preferred full-time employment but were\nL229:     working part time because their hours had been reduced or they were unable to find full-time\nL230:     jobs. (See table A-8.)\nL231: \nL232:     In August, the number of people not in the labor force who currently want a job changed little\nL233:     at 5.7 million. These individuals were not counted as unemployed because they were not\nL234:     actively looking for work during the 4 weeks preceding the survey or were unavailable to take\nL235:     a job. (See table A-1.)\nL236: \nL237:     Among those not in the labor force who wanted a job, the number of people marginally attached\nL238:     to the labor force changed little at 1.7 million in August. These individuals wanted and were\nL239:     available for work and had looked for a job sometime in the prior 12 months but had not looked\nL240:     for work in the 4 weeks preceding the survey. The number of discouraged workers, a subset of\nL241:     the marginally attached who believed that no jobs were available for them, changed little in\nL242:     August at 441,000. (See Summary table A.)\nL243: \nL244:     Establishment Survey Data\nL245: \nL246:     Total nonfarm payroll employment rose by 162,000 in August, higher than the average monthly\nL247:     gain of 31,000 over the prior 12 months. In August, employment increased in food services and\nL248:     drinking places and in local government education. The information industry lost jobs.\nL249:     (See table B-1.)\nL250: \nL251:     Employment in food services and drinking places increased by 59,000 in August, well above the\nL252:     average monthly gain of 12,000 over the prior 12 months.\nL253: \nL254:     Local government education added 42,000 jobs in August, largely offsetting a decrease in the\nL255:     prior month. Local government education has shown little net change since January 2025.\nL256: \nL257:     In August, employment in manufacturing continued its upward trend (+16,000) and is up by\nL258:     58,000 since a recent low in December 2025. Employment in machinery manufacturing (+6,000) and\nL259:     in fabricated metal product manufacturing (+6,000) continued trending up in August.\nL260: \nL261:     Employment in health care continued to trend up in August (+13,000) but at a slower pace than\nL262:     the average monthly gain over the prior 12 months (+32,000). Over the month, home health care\nL263:     services (+11,000) and hospitals (+8,000) added jobs.\nL264: \nL265:     Information employment declined by 23,000 in August, following losses that had averaged 8,000\nL266:     per month over the prior 12 months. In August, job losses occurred in computing infrastructure\nL267:     providers, data processing, web hosting, and related services (-8,000), in publishing\nL268:     industries (-7,000), and in broadcasting and content providers (-5,000).\nL269: \nL270:     Construction employment changed little in August (+22,000). Employment in nonresidential\nL271:     specialty trade contractors continued to trend up (+8,000), similar to the average monthly\nL272:     gain over the prior 12 months (+6,000).\nL273: \nL274:     Employment showed little change over the month in other major industries, including mining,\nL275:     quarrying, and oil and gas extraction; wholesale trade; retail trade; transportation and\nL276:     warehousing; financial activities; professional and business services; social assistance; and\nL277:     other services.\nL278: \nL279:     In August, average hourly earnings for all employees on private nonfarm payrolls rose by 10\nL280:     cents, or 0.3 percent, to $37.75. Over the year, average hourly earnings have increased by 3.1\nL281:     percent. In August, average hourly earnings of private-sector production and nonsupervisory\nL282:     employees rose by 11 cents, or 0.3 percent, to $32.53. (See tables B-3 and B-8.)\nL283:     The average workweek for all employees on private nonfarm payrolls edged up by 0.1 hour to\nL284:     34.4 hours in August. In manufacturing, the average workweek edged up by 0.1 hour to 40.5\nL285:     hours, and overtime was unchanged at 3.1 hours. The average workweek for production and\nL286:     nonsupervisory employees on private nonfarm payrolls remained at 33.8 hours.\nL287:     (See tables B-2 and B-7.)\nL288: \nL289:     The change in total nonfarm payroll employment for June was revised up by 11,000, from\nL290:     +20,000 to +31,000, and the change for July was revised up by 44,000, from -23,000 to +21,000.\nL291:     With these revisions, employment in June and July combined is 55,000 higher than previously\nL292:     reported. (Monthly revisions result from additional reports received from businesses and\nL293:     government agencies since the last published estimates and from the recalculation of seasonal\nL294:     factors.)\nL295: \nL296:     _____________\nL297:     The Employment Situation for September 2026 is scheduled to be published on Friday,\nL298:     October 2, 2026, at 8:30 a.m. (ET).\nL299: HOUSEHOLD DATA\nL300: Summary table A. Household data, seasonally adjusted [Numbers in thousands]\nL301: Category  | Aug.\nL302: 2025  | June\nL303: 2026  | July\nL304: 2026  | Aug.\nL305: 2026  | Change from:\nL306: July\nL307: 2026-\nL308: Aug.\nL309: 2026\nL310: --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nL311: Employment status  |\nL312: Civilian noninstitutional population(cite153†1 )  | 274,001  | 275,166  | 275,282  | 275,415  | 133\nL313: Civilian labor force  | 170,750  | 169,358  | 169,094  | 169