nd logging and manufacturing, construction employees in construction, and nonsupervisory employees in the service-providing industries.\nL413: (3) The indexes of aggregate weekly hours are calculated by dividing the current month's estimates of aggregate hours by the corresponding annual average aggregate hours.\nL414: (4) The indexes of aggregate weekly payrolls are calculated by dividing the current month's estimates of aggregate weekly payrolls by the corresponding annual average aggregate weekly payrolls.\nL415: (5) Figures are the percent of industries with employment increasing plus one-half of the industries with unchanged employment, where 50 percent indicates an equal balance between industries with increasing and decreasing employment.\nL416: (p) Preliminary\nL417: NOTE: Data have been revised to reflect March 2025 benchmark levels and updated seasonal adjustment factors.\nL418: \nL419:     Frequently Asked Questions about Employment and Unemployment Estimates\nL420: \nL421:     1. Why are there two monthly measures of employment?\nL422: \nL423:        The household survey and establishment survey both produce sample-based estimates\nL424:        of employment, and both have strengths and limitations. The establishment survey\nL425:        employment series has a smaller margin of error on the measurement of month-to-\nL426:        month change than the household survey because of its much larger sample size. An\nL427:        over-the-month employment change of about 122,000 is statistically significant in\nL428:        the establishment survey, while the threshold for a statistically significant change\nL429:        in the household survey is about 650,000. However, the household survey has a more\nL430:        expansive scope than the establishment survey because it includes self-employed\nL431:        workers whose businesses are unincorporated, unpaid family workers, agricultural\nL432:        workers, and private household workers, who are excluded by the establishment survey.\nL433:        The household survey also provides estimates of employment for demographic groups.\nL434:        For more information on the differences between the two surveys, please visit\nL435:        www.bls.gov/web/empsit/ces_cps_trends.htm.\nL436:     2. Are undocumented immigrants counted in the surveys?\nL437: \nL438:        It is likely that both surveys include at least some undocumented immigrants. However,\nL439:        neither the establishment nor the household survey is designed to identify the legal\nL440:        status of workers. Therefore, it is not possible to determine how many are counted in\nL441:        either survey. The establishment survey does not collect data on the legal status of\nL442:        workers. The household survey does include questions which identify the foreign and\nL443:        native born, but it does not include questions about the legal status of the foreign\nL444:        born. Data on the foreign and native born are published each month in table A-7 of\nL445:        The Employment Situation news release.\nL446: \nL447:     3. Why does the establishment survey have revisions?\nL448: \nL449:        The establishment survey revises published estimates to improve its data series by\nL450:        incorporating additional information that was not available at the time of the\nL451:        initial publication of the estimates. The establishment survey revises its initial\nL452:        monthly estimates twice, in the immediately succeeding 2 months, to incorporate\nL453:        additional sample receipts from respondents in the survey and recalculated seasonal\nL454:        adjustment factors. For more information on the monthly revisions, please visit\nL455:        www.bls.gov/web/empsit/cestn.htm#Revisions-Between-Preliminary-and-Final-Data.\nL456: \nL457:        On an annual basis, the establishment survey incorporates a benchmark revision that\nL458:        re-anchors estimates to nearly complete employment counts available from unemployment\nL459:        insurance tax records. The benchmark helps to control for sampling and modeling errors\nL460:        in the estimates. For more information on the annual benchmark revision, please visit\nL461:        www.bls.gov/web/empsit/cesbmart.htm.\nL462:     4. Does the establishment survey sample include small firms?\nL463: \nL464:        Yes. About 46 percent of the establishment survey sample is comprised of business\nL465:        establishments with fewer than 20 employees. The establishment survey sample is\nL466:        designed to maximize the reliability of the statewide total nonfarm employment\n"
